  "difficulty": 2,
  "module_type": "standard",
  "question_text": "In the figure shown, circle O has points A and B on it. Point P lies outside the circle, and segments PA and PB are tangent to the circle at A and B, respectively (segments PA and PB are not drawn in the figure). It is given that PA = 6√3 centimeters and that angle APB measures 60°. What is the area, in square centimeters, of quadrilateral OAPB?",
  "answer_a": "18√3",
  "answer_b": "54",
  "answer_c": "36√3",
  "answer_d": "72√3",
  "correct_answer": "C",
  "explanation": "Choice C is correct. Tangent segments drawn to a circle from the same external point are congruent, so PA = PB = 6√3. A radius drawn to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent, so triangle OAP is a right triangle with the right angle at A. Because OP bisects angle APB, angle APO measures 30°, so triangle OAP is a 30-60-90 triangle in which the leg OA is opposite the 30° angle. Then tan 30° = OA/PA, so OA = 6√3 · (1/√3) = 6, and the radius is 6 centimeters. Quadrilateral OAPB is made of two congruent right triangles, OAP and OBP, each with legs 6 and 6√3. The area of one triangle is (1/2)(6)(6√3) = 18√3, so the area of the quadrilateral is 2(18√3) = 36√3 square centimeters. Therefore, the area of quadrilateral OAPB is 36√3.",
  "distractor_a_explanation": "Choice A is incorrect. This is the area of only one of the two congruent right triangles, OAP, not the area of the whole quadrilateral OAPB, which consists of triangles OAP and OBP.",
  "distractor_b_explanation": "Choice B is incorrect. This may result from treating PA = 6√3 as the hypotenuse of the 30-60-90 triangle and applying sine instead of tangent, which gives OA = 6√3 · sin 30° = 3√3. The area of the two triangles would then be 2 · (1/2)(3√3)(6√3) = 54.",
  "distractor_c_explanation": "",
  "distractor_d_explanation": "Choice D is incorrect. This may result from correctly finding OP = 12 and AB = 6√3 but then calculating the area of the quadrilateral as the product of its diagonals, 12 · 6√3 = 72√3, without multiplying by 1/2.",
