  "difficulty": 2,
  "module_type": "standard",
  "question_text": "A city park has a circular running track. The track is the region between two concentric circles, which have the same center O. A straight fence, represented by chord AB of the larger circle, is 28 meters long and touches the smaller circle at exactly one point. In the figure shown, only the larger circle, its center O, and chord AB are drawn; the smaller circle is not shown. What is the area, in square meters, of the track?",
  "answer_a": "14π",
  "answer_b": "28π",
  "answer_c": "196π",
  "answer_d": "784π",
  "correct_answer": "C",
  "explanation": "Choice C is correct. Let R be the radius of the larger circle and r be the radius of the smaller circle. Since chord AB touches the smaller circle at exactly one point, AB is tangent to the smaller circle. The radius of the smaller circle drawn to the point of tangency is perpendicular to AB, and a perpendicular from the center of a circle to a chord bisects the chord. So the point of tangency is the midpoint M of AB, and AM = 28/2 = 14. In right triangle OMA, OM = r, OA = R, and AM = 14, so by the Pythagorean theorem, r^2 + 14^2 = R^2, or R^2 - r^2 = 196. The area of the track is the area of the larger circle minus the area of the smaller circle, which is πR^2 - πr^2 = π(R^2 - r^2) = 196π. Therefore, the area of the track is 196π square meters.",
  "distractor_a_explanation": "Choice A is incorrect. This is π times the half-chord length, 14, which results from not squaring the length when applying the area formula.",
  "distractor_b_explanation": "Choice B is incorrect. This is 2π times the half-chord length, 14, which results from using the circumference formula 2πr instead of an area formula.",
  "distractor_c_explanation": "",
  "distractor_d_explanation": "Choice D is incorrect. This is π times the square of the full chord length, 28, which results from using the full chord instead of the half-chord as the leg of the right triangle.",
